    <DataNode elementId="AT5G50600-MONOMER" textLabel="11β-, 17β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase / 17β-ketosteroid reductase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9LUF1" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="450" centerY="335" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">HSD1 is a member of the short-chain dehydrogenase / reductase family of enzymes that associates with seed oil bodies in Arabidopsis thaliana |CITS: [17074428][19542545][17616511]|. Several different assays have revealed the multifunctional nature of this protein. An &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; assay using purified recombinant GST:(&amp;Delta;1-33)HSD1 (lacking an N-terminal hydrophobic extension) demonstrated the ability of this enzyme to convert |FRAME: CPD-352 estradiol-17&amp;beta;| to |FRAME: ESTRONE estrone|, indicative of 17&amp;beta;-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase activity. This activity was confirmed using an oil body fraction generated from Arabidopsis seeds. In addition, this fraction showed 11&amp;beta;-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase activity through the action of the putative HSD1 on |FRAME: CORTISOL cortisol| and |FRAME: CORTICOSTERONE corticosterone| |CITS: [17074428]|. The enzyme prep from the oil bodies also showed 17&amp;beta;-hydroxysteroid ketoreductase activity through its ability to reduce |FRAME: ESTRONE estrone| |CITS: [17074428]|. The enzyme was not active against |FRAME: CHOLESTEROL cholesterol| or |FRAME: 3-BETA-HYDROXYANDROST-5-EN-17-ONE 3-&amp;beta;-hydroxyandrost-5-en-17-one| In general, HSD1 appeared to prefer NADPH as a cofactor. To date, the biologically relevant substrates and products for this enzyme have not been found. However, overexpression of this enzyme can lead to increased plant growth and altered brassinosteroid and abscisic acid responses. Transcript levels for this enzyme rise in response to |FRAME: CPD-697 brassinolide| |CITS: [17616511]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT5G50600-MONOMER" />
